@meta
id: 28-03
title: Group the pile into sections
chapter: 28
type: lesson
skills: vimrc-structure
par: 106
minutes: 7
vscode: settings.json, where everything is alphabetical and nothing is grouped
open: .vimrc

@brief
Fifteen settings added one at a time, in the order things annoyed you,
is a pile. You will read this file for years, usually in a hurry,
usually to answer "did I set that, or is it a default?". So it gets
sections, and each section gets a comment.

The eight settings under the header block belong in three groups, in
this order:

    " --- Looks ---
    set number
    set ruler
    set showcmd

    " --- Searching ---
    set incsearch
    set hlsearch

    " --- Indentation ---
    set expandtab
    set shiftwidth=2
    set autoindent

Nothing is added and nothing is removed. The lines move and three
comments appear. The top three lines stay exactly where they are.

Moving lines is chapter 24 work: `:m` takes a range in front and a
destination line after it, and the cursor never has to go near either.

@keys
| `:10m7` | move line 10 to sit below line 7 |
| `O` | open a line above and type |
| `Enter` in insert | start another line without leaving insert mode |
| `:m` | move the current line |

@hints
Sort the lines first, add the comment headers afterwards. Doing it the
other way round means your line numbers change under you twice.
--
`ruler` needs to sit below `number`, and `showcmd` below `ruler`. Both
are `:m` with a line number. Check the gutter — `set number` is on, so
the numbers are right there.
--
`:10m7`, `:13m8`, `:12m10` puts the eight in order. Then `8G` and `O`
for the first header; for the other two, `O` followed by Enter gives you
a blank line and then the comment.

@solution
    :10m7<Enter> :13m8<Enter> :12m10<Enter>
    7G O " --- Looks --- <Esc>
    4j O <Enter> " --- Searching --- <Esc>
    3j O <Enter> " --- Indentation --- <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Three moves, three inserts, around 100 keystrokes. Retyping the eight
lines would cost twice that and would introduce a typo in an option
name that vim would then complain about at every startup.

The grouping is not decoration. It is the difference between a config
you can bisect and one you cannot. When something is wrong, you comment
out a section, restart, and either the problem is gone or it is not —
that halves the search space in one step. Lesson 28-15 does exactly
that. A file with no seams has to be bisected line by line.

Use whatever section names mean something to you. The ones people
converge on are looks, searching, indentation, editing, backups,
mappings and filetypes, roughly in that order, because that is also the
order in which they stop being defaults and start being yours.

@start .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

set number
set incsearch
set expandtab
set ruler
set hlsearch
set shiftwidth=2
set showcmd
set autoindent

@expected .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Looks ---
set number
set ruler
set showcmd

" --- Searching ---
set incsearch
set hlsearch

" --- Indentation ---
set expandtab
set shiftwidth=2
set autoindent
